UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53640101125740
DTSTART:20261015T140000Z
DTEND:20261016T000000Z
DESCRIPTION:We invite you to the exhibition “Friendly Arbitration: Honoré Daumier on Crime\, Justice\, and Punishment” on view at MoFA until Saturday\, December 12. Friendly Arbitration examines Honoré Daumier’s (1808–1879) subversive use of lithography and wood engraving as tools for artistic innovation and social critique in nineteenth-century France. Bringing together original prints from his popular series on lawyers\, the legal system\, and crime and punishment\, the exhibition explores how his satirical caricatures challenged judicial authority\, exposed social inequities\, and helped reshape the fields of art and journalism.\n\nThis exhibition is made possible through the generous support of Jay Steele & Matthew Wright.
GEO:30.444141;-84.29018
LOCATION:Museum of Fine Arts 
SUMMARY:Friendly Arbitration: Honoré Daumier on Crime\, Justice\, and Punishment
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.fsu.edu/event/friendly-arbitration-honore-daumier-on-crime-justice-and-punishment
CATEGORIES:Arts/Entertainment
CATEGORIES:Free to the Public
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20261015T235231Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54171092116714
DTSTART:20261015T180000Z
DTEND:20261015T230000Z
DESCRIPTION:This October 14 and 15\, FSU Libraries and the FSU Museum of Fine Arts (MoFA) will come together in concert with the MoFA’s Friendly Arbitration: Honoré Daumier on Crime\, Justice\, and Punishment\, a major exhibition of prints by Honoré Daumier (1808–1879)\, whose innovative use of lithography helped transform print into a powerful vehicle for artistic expression\, social critique\, and mass communication in 19th century France. By making visual culture more accessible to broad audiences\, Daumier's work provides a compelling foundation for conversations about print culture\, information dissemination\, visual literacy\, and public discourse.\n\nThis two-day interdisciplinary event brings together FSU Libraries and the FSU Museum of Fine Arts to explore the history\, culture\, and significance of print media through scholarship\, collections\, artistic practice\, and public engagement.\n\n \n\nEVENT SCHEDULE\n\nWEDNESDAY\, October 14th\, Bradley Room\, Strozier Library \n\nIntroduction/Welcome: 1:00 PM \n\nSession 1:1:05 PM \nProf. Denise Bookwalter (Art)  \nDr. Michelle Bumatay (Modern Languages)  \n\nShort Break: 2:30 PM \n\nSession 2: 2:45 PM \nDr. Lindsey Eckert (English)  \nDr. Sonia Hazard (Religion)  \n\nRemarks: 4:10 PM \nDistinguished scholar Dr. Patrick Rowe  \n(Collector/Independent Curator)  \n\nDessert & Coffee Reception: 4:30 PM \n\n \n\nTHURSDAY\, October 15th\, FSU Museum of Fine Arts (MoFA) \n\nArt in the Library x Make it with MoFA: Pronto Plate Lithography: 2:00 PM - 5:00 PM \n\nReception and Curator Tours: 5:00 PM - 7:00 PM \n\n \n\nThis event is only possible thanks to the collaboration between FSU Libraries and the Museum of Fine Arts.
GEO:30.444141;-84.29018
LOCATION:Museum of Fine Arts \, Lower Gallery
SUMMARY:"Friendly Arbitration: Honoré Daumier on Crime\, Justice\, and Punishment" Symposium
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.fsu.edu/event/friendly-arbitration-honore-daumier-on-crime-justice-and-punishment-symposium
